
(verb :name "with-exception-handler" :std "R7RS-small" :library "exception" :tier "core" :kind "meta" :signature "(with-exception-handler handler thunk) -> value"
  :summary "R7RS §6.11 — run thunk; if it raises, call handler on the raised value and return what the handler returns."
  :explanation "Two procedures of no interest on their own become one guarded computation. `thunk` is the work; `handler` is what to say when the work fails. If the thunk finishes, its value is the value of the whole form and the handler is never called. If the thunk raises — via `error`, `raise`, `escalate`, or a runtime fault like taking the car of the empty list — the handler is called with the raised value and ITS return value becomes the value of the whole form. That last sentence is the part to read twice. In strict R7RS a handler that returns normally from a non-continuable `raise` is itself an error, and portable code reaches for `guard` to recover. Motoi does not draw that line: the handler returning IS the recovery, so `with-exception-handler` here does the job `guard` does elsewhere. Code written against Motoi's reading will run on another Scheme in a way you did not intend, and that is worth knowing before you port it."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(with-exception-handler (lambda (e) 'caught) (lambda () (error \"boom\")))" :note "caught — the thunk raised, so the handler ran and its value is the value of the whole form.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(with-exception-handler (lambda (e) 'caught) (lambda () (* 6 7)))" :note "42 — nothing raised, so the handler was never called and the thunk's own value came back.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (error-object-message e)) (lambda () (error \"disk full\")))" :note "\"disk full\" — the handler receives the condition object, so it can read the message off it.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(with-exception-handler (lambda (outer) (list 'outer outer)) (lambda () (with-exception-handler (lambda (inner) (raise 'again)) (lambda () (raise 'first)))))" :note "(outer again) — a handler may raise; the raise travels to the NEXT handler out, not back into its own.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(define (attempt thunk fallback)\n  (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) fallback) thunk))\n(display (list (attempt (lambda () (/ 10 2)) 'failed) (attempt (lambda () (error \"nope\")) 'failed)))\n(newline)" :note "(5 failed) — one wrapper, two outcomes: the value when the work succeeds, the fallback when it does not."))
  :caveats ("The handler's return value becomes the value of the form. R7RS says a handler returning from a non-continuable raise is an error and expects `guard` for recovery; Motoi deliberately accepts the return. Portable code should not rely on this." "The handler is NOT called for a thunk that finishes normally, even if the thunk returned #f or an error-looking value. Only a raise reaches it." "The handler runs after the thunk's stack is already unwound; there is no way to resume the thunk from where it raised." "Runtime faults raise too — (car '()), (vector-ref v 99) and an unbound symbol all reach the handler, not just explicit calls to error." "A handler that raises escapes to the enclosing handler, so an outer with-exception-handler is the only thing that will catch it.")
  :usecases ("Supplying a fallback value when a computation may fail." "Reading the message or irritants off a condition to build a report." "Retrying: the handler calls the operation again with a smaller count." "Stopping a pipeline at the first stage that refuses its input.")
  :related ("error" "raise" "raise-continuable" "escalate" "error-object-message" "error-object-irritants" "error-object-type")
  :learn (:concept "A guarded computation is two procedures: the work and what to say when the work fails. The handler's answer stands in for the work's answer." :prerequisites ("lambda" "error" "raise") :progression "Start with a constant handler that returns a fallback, then read the condition with error-object-message, then let the handler call the operation again to build a retry.")
  :provenance "R7RS §6.11; implementation lib/base/r7rs-small.js:981 (handler return accepted — see caveats); reference record added 2026-08-02"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (load-config raw)\n  (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (list 'defaults))\n    (lambda () (if (string? raw) (list 'parsed raw) (error \"config is not text\" raw)))))\n(display (list (load-config \"depth=3\") (load-config 42)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (safe-recip x)\n  (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) 'undefined) (lambda () (if (zero? x) (error \"no reciprocal of zero\") (/ 1 x)))))\n(display (map safe-recip (list 4 0 -2)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (attempt thunk fallback) (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) fallback) thunk))\n(display (list (attempt (lambda () (vector-ref (vector 1 2 3) 1)) 'oops) (attempt (lambda () (car '())) 'oops)))\n(newline)"
     :problem "A cart reads a config value that may be missing or the wrong shape, and it must still boot. Candidates: check every field with a predicate before touching it (correct, but the checks outnumber the work and a new field silently escapes them), let it crash (the cart dies on a typo), or run the parse inside a handler that answers with defaults."
     :explanation "eng: the parse either produces (parsed …) or the handler produces (defaults). math: reciprocal is undefined at zero, so the handler names the gap instead of pretending. cs: the same wrapper catches an explicit error and a runtime fault — (car '()) never called error, and the handler still got it."
     :meta "Reach for with-exception-handler and NOT a pre-flight predicate when the failures are open-ended. A predicate only guards the case you thought of; the handler guards every raise inside the thunk, including ones the engine raised on your behalf.")
    (:eng "One boot path, not two: the cart always gets a config, and the log line says which kind." :math "A partial function made total by naming its gap, rather than by returning a number that is not an answer." :cs "The thunk boundary is the try block; the handler is the catch. What is new is that the catch's value IS the expression's value."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Do the risky thing inside a boundary that has already been told what to say if the risky thing refuses."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "exception" :code "(with-exception-handler (lambda (e) 'fallback) (lambda () (error \"sensor offline\")))")
       (:domain "a sensor reading with a last-known-good value" :code "(display (map (lambda (x) (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) 'undefined) (lambda () (if (negative? x) (error \"outside domain\" x) (sqrt x))))) (list 9 -1 16)))\n(newline)")
       (:domain "a partial function extended by naming the gap" :code "(display (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (list 'recovered (error-object-message e))) (lambda () (error \"stage 2 failed\"))))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "A sensor that cannot read reports its last-known-good value rather than nothing; a partial function extended to a total one answers 'undefined' rather than nothing; a guarded thunk answers with the handler rather than nothing. All three keep the caller's contract — one value, always — by deciding in advance what the failure case is worth."
     :meta "The shape is total-by-construction. Wherever a caller cannot cope with 'no answer', someone upstream has to pick the answer that failure means. The handler is where that pick is written down.")
    (:eng "The fallback is a decision, so put it where a reader will find it — at the boundary, not buried at the raise site." :math "Extending a partial function to a total one requires choosing a value for the gap; the handler is that choice made explicit." :cs "Same family as Maybe/Either with a default, or a promise's .catch — recover by supplying the other branch."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(display (list 'no-raise (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) 'H) (lambda () 'BODY)) 'raise (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) 'H) (lambda () (error \"x\") 'BODY))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) 'handler-value) (lambda () (error \"any\"))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) 'never) (lambda () (+ 1 2))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "The form has exactly two outcomes and no third: the thunk's value if it returns, the handler's value if it raises. Code in the thunk after a raise never runs."
     :invariant "For all handler h, thunk t. with-exception-handler(h,t) = t() when t returns; = h(v) when t raises v; and no suffix of t after the raise is evaluated"
     :meta "The first program is the whole proof in one line: same handler, same body expression, one raise added, and the answer flips from BODY to H. What would falsify it: a run where BODY came back even though the raise happened — which is what an engine that resumed the thunk would produce. Motoi does not resume, so it cannot."
    )
    (:eng "Two outcomes is a testable contract; a third would mean the boundary leaks." :math "Total function on the pair (handler, thunk) — every input has exactly one result." :cs "The unreachable-suffix property is what makes raise a non-local exit rather than a return value."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (retry n thunk)\n  (if (= n 0) 'gave-up (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (retry (- n 1) thunk)) thunk)))\n(define tries 0)\n(display (retry 3 (lambda () (set! tries (+ tries 1)) (if (< tries 3) (error \"flaky\") (list 'ok-after tries)))))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (first-defined xs f)\n  (if (null? xs) 'none (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (first-defined (cdr xs) f)) (lambda () (f (car xs))))))\n(display (first-defined (list -4 -1 25) (lambda (x) (if (negative? x) (error \"outside domain\") (sqrt x)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (guarded-pipeline v stages)\n  (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (list 'stopped (error-object-message e)))\n    (lambda () (fold (lambda (stage acc) (stage acc)) v stages))))\n(display (guarded-pipeline 2 (list (lambda (x) (* x 10)) (lambda (x) (if (> x 15) (error \"too big\" x) x)))))\n(newline)"
     :base "with-exception-handler turns a raise into a value chosen by the handler."
     :emergent "eng: a retry loop — the handler is the recursive call, so failure is just another iteration. math: search-until-defined — walk the inputs and stop at the first one the function accepts. cs: a pipeline with one guard around the whole fold, so the first stage that refuses names itself and the rest never run."
     :meta "The move that generates all three is putting a RECURSIVE call in the handler. Once the handler can do work, failure stops being a dead end and becomes a branch of the control flow — retry, next-candidate, and fail-fast are the same construction with a different next step."
    )
    (:eng "The retry counter lives in one place and the flaky operation does not know it is being retried." :math "Search-until-defined is the domain of a partial function computed by trial rather than by predicate." :cs "One guard around the fold beats one guard per stage: the failing stage identifies itself through the message."))
)
